{"id": 25, "ts": 1784214633.470252, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 2, "agent": "agent_1", "message": "I agree with the earlier point that Candidate\u202fA\u2019s 100\u202f% reliability and his ability to create a positive crew atmosphere are strong assets for long\u2011distance operations. However, we still lack critical evidence on his stress\u2011resilience, depth of decision\u2011making, and technical competence\u2014criteria that are equally vital for safety\u2011critical flights.\nAt the same time, Candidate\u202fC brings a different set of strengths: he can anticipate dangerous situations, sees complex connections, has excellent spatial vision, and demonstrates very good leadership qualities. These traits suggest strong technical and situational awareness, which could translate into high\u2011quality decisions under pressure. Yet, we also know C can be unorganized and sometimes struggles with criticism, raising concerns about adaptability and crew cooperation.\nGiven that we have no concrete data on either A\u2019s or C\u2019s performance under stress or decision quality, I propose we request any available observations on Candidate\u202fC\u2019s stress resilience and decision quality to better compare him with A. This will help us determine whether C\u2019s technical strengths outweigh his cooperation concerns, or whether A\u2019s proven reliability and crew atmosphere remain the more balanced choice."}
